{
  // Every fence in this repo lives in THIS file and nowhere else. oxlint resolves the nearest
  // config, so a second .oxlintrc.json anywhere below the root would replace this one for its whole
  // subtree and leave `pnpm lint` green. The three oxlint scripts therefore pin
  // `--config .oxlintrc.json`, which makes a nested config inert, and `pnpm check:oxlint-config`
  // reports both a stray config and a script that lost the pin. Narrow a rule with an "overrides"
  // entry below, never with a nested config file — and never with a .gitignore line either. oxlint
  // and oxfmt both walk the tree honoring gitignore at any depth, so a gitignore line over a
  // tracked source file removes it from linting AND formatting while the file still ships; the pin
  // does not reach that, so `pnpm check:oxlint-config` reports it by name and ignore line. An "overrides"
  // entry REPLACES a rule's options wholesale, so every scoped fence below is a verbatim COPY of the root
  // list: restate each root group byte-identically (group array AND message), or exempt this scope from one
  // with an `oxlint-omission` marker line beside that entry's own rule key — the marker names one specifier
  // of the omitted group and then a reason, and the two below are the pattern. `check:oxlint-config` asserts
  // both directions, including a marker that has gone stale or vacuous. Do not spell a marker in prose up
  // here: this header is the ROOT site's own region, and the root may not exempt itself from its own group.
  "plugins": null,
  "jsPlugins": [
    "oxlint-tailwindcss",
    "./scripts/oxlint/anti-slop/index.ts",
    "./scripts/oxlint/alfred/index.ts"
  ],
  "categories": {},
  "options": {
    "typeAware": true
  },
  "rules": {
    // The vendored anti-slop rules (scripts/oxlint/anti-slop/, see its README for the
    // upstream commits and for the upstream rules this repo does not vendor).
    //
    // Eleven rules at `error`. Each was either adopted clean (zero violations on arrival,
    // so it can only fire on code written after adoption) or driven to zero at warn and
    // promoted here. That is also the bar for registering a new rule — a rule the tree
    // already violates belongs in its own change, violations first, rule second.
    // require-readable-spacing is the one autofix rule: adopted by running its
    // whitespace-only `--fix` across the tree to zero, so the diff added blank lines
    // and nothing else.
    // Four rules were promoted after a by-surface scoping: product source was
    // driven to zero and the test/eval scopes, the two ops-scripts trees
    // (apps/server/src/scripts, packages/*/src/scripts), plus named
    // honest-boundary files where the flagged spelling IS the boundary parse,
    // are exempted by "overrides" entries at the bottom of this file. Repo-level
    // scripts/ gates are NOT exempt — each carries its own per-site disable. See
    // docs/research/anti-slop-paydown-2026-09-12.md for the promotion bar.
    // (no-unsafe-dictionary-type, no-unknown-returns, and no-runtime-typeof.)
    "anti-slop/no-module-mocking": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-reflect-apply": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-widen-then-assert": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-object-parameters": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-chained-type-assertions": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-known-value-widening": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-unknown-type-aliases": "error",
    "anti-slop/require-readable-spacing": "error",
    // No rule remains at warn for paydown. Three upstream rules stay out:
    // - no-conditional-empty-object-spread: conflicts with exactOptionalPropertyTypes
    // - no-unknown-parameters: conflicts with boundary validator pattern
    // - no-reflect-get: conflicts with Reflect.get for class instances
    // no-shape-in-symbol-names was dropped in #1149: a substring ban cannot tell
    // the credential `shape` field from the DayShape domain, so it is documented
    // in docs/reference/code-style.md instead of enforced.
    // allowInTypeGuards: `typeof` inside a `value is T` predicate is the predicate's
    // correct implementation, not the ad-hoc narrowing this rule exists to reject.
    "anti-slop/no-runtime-typeof": ["error", { "allowInTypeGuards": true }],
    "anti-slop/no-unknown-returns": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-unsafe-dictionary-type": "error",
    // Alfred's own rules (scripts/oxlint/alfred/, first-party, see its README). All but one
    // were regex gates in scripts/consolidation-rules.mjs that needed scope analysis to stop
    // missing cases and flagging look-alikes; no-exported-hasher was born here. Each keeps the
    // gate's file scope by itself (`inGateScope` reads `isSkippedPath`), and a rule that had
    // per-rule `owners` keeps them as a closed list in its own source, so none needs an
    // "overrides" entry here.
    // no-function-wrapper-shim: an exported function that only returns a constant or an
    // imported name. sha256-over-json-stringify: a hash digest over JSON.stringify output,
    // whose pre-image depends on key order; use sha256Canonical. no-exported-hasher: a
    // function outside @alfred/db/hash that is exported and hashes its parameter, which
    // keeps the set of cross-file hashers closed for the sha256 rule.
    // no-constants-re-export / no-type-re-export-shim: a logic file that hands out a value /
    // a type of a `constants` module again; only the package barrel may. no-type-alias-shim:
    // an exported type that is only a new name for an imported type.
    "alfred/no-constants-re-export": "error",
    "alfred/no-exported-hasher": "error",
    "alfred/no-function-wrapper-shim": "error",
    "alfred/no-type-alias-shim": "error",
    "alfred/no-type-re-export-shim": "error",
    "alfred/sha256-over-json-stringify": "error",
    // The tailwind rules sit at `error` by the same bar: zero violations on promotion, and
    // `pnpm lint` does not fail on `warn`, so a warn-level rule here enforced nothing.
    // `pnpm check:oxlint-plugin` drives only the anti-slop and alfred rules, not these.
    // enforce-canonical landed with the tree autofixed to zero; the built CSS kept every
    // declaration block. One directive keeps a `[&_…]:` variant group in one spelling.
    "tailwindcss/enforce-sort-order": ["error", { "entryPoint": "apps/web/src/index.css" }],
    "tailwindcss/enforce-shorthand": "error",
    "tailwindcss/no-deprecated-classes": ["error", { "entryPoint": "apps/web/src/index.css" }],
    "tailwindcss/enforce-canonical": "error",
    // Syntax rules from oxlint's built-in plugins, each adopted at zero by the same bar.
    // consistent-type-imports: an import used only as a type, including only inside `typeof`,
    // takes `type`. `verbatimModuleSyntax` already fails `tsc` on a type imported without it,
    // but a value imported only for `typeof` compiles and stays in the runtime import graph.
    // `fixStyle` matches the tree: a mixed import marks each type inline. The fixer then
    // writes `import { type X }` for a declaration whose every name is a type; rewrite that as
    // `import type { X }`, because the inline form keeps a side-effect import of the module.
    // `disallowTypeAnnotations` is off: an `import("…")` type is erased, and the
    // `test/type/*.type-test.ts` fixtures need it to probe the exports map.
    "typescript/consistent-type-imports": [
      "error",
      { "disallowTypeAnnotations": false, "fixStyle": "inline-type-imports" }
    ],
    // no-useless-spread: oxlint's default set has it at `warn`, which `pnpm lint` does not
    // fail on. Its common hit here is `{ ...(x ? { k: x } : {}) }` with nothing else in the
    // literal; pass the conditional itself (docs/reference/code-style.md §2).
    "unicorn/no-useless-spread": "error",
    // Type-aware rules. `options.typeAware` above runs them through oxlint-tsgolint (the tsgo
    // compiler, a root devDependency), so every oxlint invocation — `pnpm lint`, the edit-time
    // hook, the check:oxlint-config probe — reads types with no CLI flag to keep in step. A
    // missing tsgolint binary fails the run; it does not skip these rules.
    //
    // Four rules at `error`, each driven to zero before it landed here, by the same bar as the
    // groups above. no-floating-promises names the node:test registrars as known-safe calls:
    // `test(...)` and `describe(...)` return a promise the runner owns, and it is not awaited
    // on purpose. switch-exhaustiveness-check keeps its defaults: a `default` does NOT count as
    // the exhaustive case for a union, so a union switch names every member and ends in the
    // `const _exhaustive: never` default (allowDefaultCaseForExhaustiveSwitch stays true, which
    // that default needs). requireDefaultForNonUnion stays false: an open `string` such as
    // `event.key` has no member set to prove, so an empty `default` would add nothing. A switch
    // over the ESTree node union in scripts/oxlint/ carries its own directive with the reason.
    // no-misused-promises and restrict-template-expressions keep their defaults.
    //
    // Turning type-aware on also arms every type-aware rule in oxlint's default `correctness`
    // set at `warn`, which `pnpm lint` does not fail on. Each one is set explicitly below at
    // `error`, so the run reports only the rules this file chose. The queued follow-up
    // (no-unnecessary-condition) lands the same way: tree to zero first, then the rule at
    // `error`.
    "typescript/no-floating-promises": [
      "error",
      {
        "allowForKnownSafeCalls": [
          {
            "from": "package",
            "package": "node:test",
            "name": ["describe", "it", "suite", "test"]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "typescript/no-misused-promises": "error",
    "typescript/switch-exhaustiveness-check": "error",
    "typescript/restrict-template-expressions": "error",
    // The no-unsafe-* family, each driven to zero before it landed: an `any` may not flow
    // into a return, an argument, a call, a member read, or an assignment. Type external,
    // persisted, and protocol data as `unknown` and parse it at the owning boundary
    // (CLAUDE.md) instead of letting `any` carry it. They are off for JavaScript sources
    // in the last "overrides" entry; JSDoc-typed `.mjs` is `any` by construction.
    "typescript/no-unsafe-return": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-argument": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-call": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-member-access": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-assignment": "error",
    // Six of the default `correctness` rules, adopted at zero (TypeScript and JavaScript
    // sources alike), default options: an `await` of a non-thenable, `delete` on an array
    // element, `for…in` over an array, a string passed to `setTimeout` / `new Function`,
    // unary minus on a non-number, and a default value for a binding that is never undefined.
    "typescript/await-thenable": "error",
    "typescript/no-array-delete": "error",
    "typescript/no-for-in-array": "error",
    "typescript/no-implied-eval": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-unary-minus": "error",
    "typescript/no-useless-default-assignment": "error",
    // Default `correctness` rules that had hits, each driven to zero before it landed, default
    // options: `void` on an expression that is already `void`, a spread of a string (split a
    // string into code points with `Array.from`, which states the intent), a union or
    // intersection that repeats a member, and `.sort()` with no comparator. no-duplicate-type-
    // constituents also reports `| undefined` on an optional PARAMETER; an optional property
    // keeps it, because exactOptionalPropertyTypes is on. require-array-sort-compare keeps
    // `ignoreStringArrays` on (its default): a `string[]` sorts by UTF-16 code unit, which is
    // deterministic, and `localeCompare` would change that order. The rule targets numbers,
    // tuples and objects, which a bare `.sort()` orders by their string form. It applies to
    // `.mjs` too, where a JSDoc-untyped array is `any[]` and needs the comparator.
    "typescript/no-meaningless-void-operator": "error",
    "typescript/no-misused-spread": "error",
    "typescript/no-duplicate-type-constituents": "error",
    "typescript/require-array-sort-compare": "error",
    // no-unnecessary-condition: a `?.`, `??`, truthiness check, or compare whose operand's type
    // already decides the result. Delete the guard only when a real parse established the type;
    // when the type is an assertion over unvalidated data (`jsonb.$type<X>()` rows that predate X,
    // `JSON.parse`, `res.json()`, a hand-typed frame), fix the type or parse at the boundary.
    // `while (true)` and `for (;;)` stay legal; a variable typed as the literal `true` does not.
    // Staged rollout: product source (apps/*/src, packages/*/src) is clean and at `error`. Tests,
    // ops scripts, repo scripts, and JavaScript are off in the two "overrides" entries just above
    // the JavaScript one, until their own changes clear them.
    "typescript/no-unnecessary-condition": [
      "error",
      { "allowConstantLoopConditions": "only-allowed-literals" }
    ],
    // Three more default rules that had hits, each driven to zero before it landed:
    // - unbound-method: a method read as a value loses `this`. Bind it or wrap it in an arrow;
    //   declare a member that never reads `this` as a function-typed property.
    // - no-redundant-type-constituents: a union member another member absorbs (`unknown | X`,
    //   `"a" | string`). Write the wide type and say the rest in the doc comment, derive the
    //   exact union, or keep autocomplete with `"a" | (string & {})`.
    // - no-base-to-string: `String(v)` or a template on a value that may be an object prints
    //   `[object Object]`. Narrow the value at its boundary, or pick the text you mean
    //   (`toDisplayText`, `toMessage`, `JSON.stringify` of a JSON value).
    "typescript/no-base-to-string": "error",
    "typescript/no-redundant-type-constituents": "error",
    "typescript/unbound-method": "error",
    "typescript/no-restricted-types": [
      "error",
      {
        "types": {
          "Record<string, any>": {
            "message": "Record<string, any> defeats every `unknown` guard the contracts package exists to provide. Use a parsed schema, a Record<LiteralKey, T>, or a Map<K, V>."
          }
        }
      }
    ],
    "no-restricted-imports": [
      "error",
      {
        "patterns": [
          {
            "group": ["@alfred/assistant/knowledge/internal"],
            "message": "The knowledge internal tooling door is restricted to apps/server/src/scripts/** (see packages/assistant/src/knowledge/internal.ts). Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/knowledge instead."
          },
          {
            "group": ["@alfred/integrations/google/internal"],
            "message": "The raw Gmail watch primitive is restricted to its one allowlisted friend file (see packages/integrations/src/google/internal.ts). Install a watch through installGmailWatchAndSeedCursor from @alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion, which also seeds the ingestion_state cursor; a raw installGmailWatch leaves the credential cursorless."
          },
          {
            "group": ["@alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion/internal"],
            "message": "The raw Gmail ingestion entry points are restricted to the operational scripts under apps/server/src/scripts/** and the ingestion-cursor test packages/assistant/test/gmail-ingest.test.ts (see packages/assistant/src/connections/ingestion/internal.ts). Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion instead, which drives ingestion through the BullMQ job that owns retry, burst dedup and the ingestion_state cursor."
          },
          {
            "group": ["@alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/registry"],
            "message": "The global tool catalog is restricted to packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/ (see packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/internal/registry.ts, which owns the one mutable map). Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/tool-runtime instead, whose index re-exports the catalog surface."
          },
          {
            "group": [
              "@alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/*",
              "@alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/**"
            ],
            "message": "Every module under packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/internal/ is private to packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/, not one door but the whole directory. Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/tool-runtime instead, whose index publishes the runtime surface. The narrower @alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/registry group above is deliberately kept beside this one: a specifier holding a glob is gated on package existence alone, so the literal registry specifier is the only one in this family that `pnpm check:oxlint-config` still resolves through the exports map to a git-listed file."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "settings": {
    "tailwindcss": {
      "entryPoint": "apps/web/src/index.css"
    },
    "jsx-a11y": {
      "polymorphicPropName": null,
      "components": {},
      "attributes": {}
    },
    "next": {
      "rootDir": []
    },
    "react": {
      "formComponents": [],
      "linkComponents": [],
      "version": null,
      "componentWrapperFunctions": []
    },
    "jsdoc": {
      "ignorePrivate": false,
      "ignoreInternal": false,
      "ignoreReplacesDocs": true,
      "overrideReplacesDocs": true,
      "augmentsExtendsReplacesDocs": false,
      "implementsReplacesDocs": false,
      "exemptDestructuredRootsFromChecks": false,
      "tagNamePreference": {}
    },
    "vitest": {
      "typecheck": false
    }
  },
  "env": {
    "builtin": true
  },
  "globals": {},
  // The vendored anti-slop plugin is third-party source copied in, not our code
  // (see scripts/oxlint/anti-slop/README.md). Its rules are proven by the fixtures
  // and the root-config drive in `pnpm check:oxlint-plugin`, not by linting the
  // plugin's own source. Exclude it here so our rules neither rewrite a copy we
  // later diff against upstream nor flag upstream idioms we chose to keep.
  "ignorePatterns": ["scripts/oxlint/anti-slop/**"],
  "overrides": [
    {
      "files": ["apps/server/src/scripts/**"],
      "rules": {
        // TWO deliberate omissions: the @alfred/assistant/knowledge/internal group and the
        // @alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion/internal group are both absent on purpose — this tree IS
        // the allowlist for both of those doors. Every other root group must be restated here, because an
        // override REPLACES the rule's options wholesale.
        // oxlint-omission: @alfred/assistant/knowledge/internal — apps/server/src/scripts/** IS this door's allowlist.
        // oxlint-omission: @alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion/internal — apps/server/src/scripts/** IS this door's allowlist.
        "no-restricted-imports": [
          "error",
          {
            "patterns": [
              {
                "group": ["@alfred/integrations/google/internal"],
                "message": "The raw Gmail watch primitive is restricted to its one allowlisted friend file (see packages/integrations/src/google/internal.ts). Install a watch through installGmailWatchAndSeedCursor from @alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion, which also seeds the ingestion_state cursor; a raw installGmailWatch leaves the credential cursorless."
              },
              {
                "group": ["@alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/registry"],
                "message": "The global tool catalog is restricted to packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/ (see packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/internal/registry.ts, which owns the one mutable map). Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/tool-runtime instead, whose index re-exports the catalog surface."
              },
              {
                "group": [
                  "@alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/*",
                  "@alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/**"
                ],
                "message": "Every module under packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/internal/ is private to packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/, not one door but the whole directory. Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/tool-runtime instead, whose index publishes the runtime surface. The narrower @alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/registry group above is deliberately kept beside this one: a specifier holding a glob is gated on package existence alone, so the literal registry specifier is the only one in this family that `pnpm check:oxlint-config` still resolves through the exports map to a git-listed file."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "files": ["packages/assistant/src/connections/ingestion/gmail-ingest.ts"],
      "rules": {
        // Deliberate omission: the @alfred/integrations/google/internal group is absent on purpose — this
        // file IS that door's allowlist. Every other root group must be restated here, because an override
        // REPLACES the rule's options wholesale. The characterization test that used to share this entry
        // moved into packages/integrations/test/ and now imports ../src/google/internal by relative path;
        // no-restricted-imports keys on the SPECIFIER, so a file inside the package it fences needs no
        // exemption. Every group below is byte-identical to the one it restates, so nothing was dropped
        // for the remaining file when the array shrank.
        // oxlint-omission: @alfred/integrations/google/internal — this file IS this door's allowlist.
        "no-restricted-imports": [
          "error",
          {
            "patterns": [
              {
                "group": ["@alfred/assistant/knowledge/internal"],
                "message": "The knowledge internal tooling door is restricted to apps/server/src/scripts/** (see packages/assistant/src/knowledge/internal.ts). Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/knowledge instead."
              },
              {
                "group": ["@alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion/internal"],
                "message": "The raw Gmail ingestion entry points are restricted to the operational scripts under apps/server/src/scripts/** and the ingestion-cursor test packages/assistant/test/gmail-ingest.test.ts (see packages/assistant/src/connections/ingestion/internal.ts). Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion instead, which drives ingestion through the BullMQ job that owns retry, burst dedup and the ingestion_state cursor."
              },
              {
                "group": ["@alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/registry"],
                "message": "The global tool catalog is restricted to packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/ (see packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/internal/registry.ts, which owns the one mutable map). Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/tool-runtime instead, whose index re-exports the catalog surface."
              },
              {
                "group": [
                  "@alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/*",
                  "@alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/**"
                ],
                "message": "Every module under packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/internal/ is private to packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/, not one door but the whole directory. Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/tool-runtime instead, whose index publishes the runtime surface. The narrower @alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/registry group above is deliberately kept beside this one: a specifier holding a glob is gated on package existence alone, so the literal registry specifier is the only one in this family that `pnpm check:oxlint-config` still resolves through the exports map to a git-listed file."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      // The ingestion-cursor test drives seedGmailHistoryCursorIfAbsent directly, so this file IS the
      // @alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion/internal door's second allowlist beside the scripts glob.
      // It gets its own entry rather than joining the two friend files above, because that entry's
      // deliberate omission is the google door and this test has no use for a raw installGmailWatch
      // exemption. No file matches two entries: an override REPLACES the rule's options wholesale and the
      // last match wins, so an overlap would silently drop one entry's list.
      "files": ["packages/assistant/test/gmail-ingest.test.ts"],
      "rules": {
        // Deliberate omission: the @alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion/internal group is absent on
        // purpose — this file IS that door's allowlist. Every other root group must be restated here.
        // oxlint-omission: @alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion/internal — packages/assistant/test/gmail-ingest.test.ts IS this door's second allowlist.
        "no-restricted-imports": [
          "error",
          {
            "patterns": [
              {
                "group": ["@alfred/assistant/knowledge/internal"],
                "message": "The knowledge internal tooling door is restricted to apps/server/src/scripts/** (see packages/assistant/src/knowledge/internal.ts). Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/knowledge instead."
              },
              {
                "group": ["@alfred/integrations/google/internal"],
                "message": "The raw Gmail watch primitive is restricted to its one allowlisted friend file (see packages/integrations/src/google/internal.ts). Install a watch through installGmailWatchAndSeedCursor from @alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion, which also seeds the ingestion_state cursor; a raw installGmailWatch leaves the credential cursorless."
              },
              {
                "group": ["@alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/registry"],
                "message": "The global tool catalog is restricted to packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/ (see packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/internal/registry.ts, which owns the one mutable map). Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/tool-runtime instead, whose index re-exports the catalog surface."
              },
              {
                "group": [
                  "@alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/*",
                  "@alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/**"
                ],
                "message": "Every module under packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/internal/ is private to packages/assistant/src/tool-runtime/, not one door but the whole directory. Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/tool-runtime instead, whose index publishes the runtime surface. The narrower @alfred/assistant/tool-runtime/internal/registry group above is deliberately kept beside this one: a specifier holding a glob is gated on package existence alone, so the literal registry specifier is the only one in this family that `pnpm check:oxlint-config` still resolves through the exports map to a git-listed file."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "files": ["packages/http/src/**"],
      "rules": {
        "no-restricted-imports": [
          "error",
          {
            "patterns": [
              {
                "group": ["@alfred/assistant/knowledge/internal"],
                "message": "The knowledge internal tooling door is restricted to apps/server/src/scripts/** (see packages/assistant/src/knowledge/internal.ts). Import the sanctioned contract from @alfred/assistant/knowledge instead."
              },
              {
                "group": ["@alfred/integrations/google/internal"],
                "message": "The raw Gmail watch primitive is restricted to its one allowlisted friend file (see packages/integrations/src/google/internal.ts). Install a watch through installGmailWatchAndSeedCursor from @alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion, which also seeds the ingestion_state cursor; a raw installGmailWatch leaves the credential cursorless."
              },
              {
                "group": ["@alfred/assistant/connections/ingestion/internal"],
